00:22:53.000 --> 00:23:24.000
being treated in the hospital? This is especially important because the absolute highest risk time for dying by suicide is in the weeks after a hospitalization for psychiatric risk, which maybe is a little counterintuitive. You think if we get a person into psychiatric care, they're now going to be, you know, they're sort of out of the woods, but statistically speaking, the rate of suicide death skyrockets after discharge from a hospitalization. And I don't think that's because hospital is making people
在医院治疗他们时，试图降低人们的风险？这一点尤为重要，因为死于自杀的绝对最高风险时间是在因精神风险住院后的几周内，这可能有点违反直觉。你认为如果我们让一个人接受精神科护理，他们现在就会，你知道，他们算是脱离危险了，但从统计学上讲，自杀死亡率在出院后会飙升。我不认为这是因为医院让人

44
00:23:24.000 --> 00:24:06.000
suicidal. I think what's happening is we're doing a good job in the front end. We're finding people at risk. We're getting them into the hospital. We're getting them inpatient care. But we haven't yet identified strong enough interventions. And we aren't yet doing a good enough job monitoring people when they leave. What we do for suicide, what we do for psychiatric care, for psychological care, looks very different than what we do in heart disease and stroke in other areas of medicine where we do really careful monitoring of people. And we develop interventions that tailor treatment to the person based on scans that we're doing in a continuous way, think about heart disease. And we do intensive cardiac rehab after a person leaves a hospital after our heart attack.
产生自杀念头。我认为发生的情况是，我们在前端做得很好。我们发现了高危人群。我们让他们住院。我们让他们接受住院护理。但我们还没有确定足够强大的干预措施。而且当他们离开时，我们还没有做好足够的监测工作。我们为自杀所做的、我们为精神科护理、心理护理所做的，看起来与我们在心脏病、中风和其他医学领域所做的非常不同，在那些领域，我们对人们进行非常仔细的监测。我们开发干预措施，根据我们持续进行的扫描来为个人量身定制治疗方案，想想心脏病。我们在心脏病发作后，在一个人离开医院后进行密集的康复治疗。

45
00:24:06.000 --> 00:25:05.000
for suicide, what we do is hospitalize someone, and then when they leave, we make a recommendation that they follow up and get outpatient care, see a psychologist, see a social worker, see a psychiatrist, but there's not the same level of follow-up that there is for other areas of medicine. So this is something that we're trying to get better at. Let's talk for a moment about technology. I know a lot of your work has focused on the use of technology and suicide prediction and prevention. Tell us more about that work. I mean, what kind of studies have you done and what are you finding? Here, too, I'm really excited. I think it's a really exciting time in our ability to understand, predict, and prevent suicide because of the kinds of technologies that are being developed. So I've been working in this area for 25, 30 years. And for all of this time and for many years, I know before, we've been sort of wringing our hands. Sorry, this is a roundabout answer to your short question. Ringing your hands because there's some challenging aspects of suicide. It's a low base rate behavior. So although it's a leading cause.
对于自杀，我们所做的是让某人住院，然后当他们离开时，我们建议他们跟进并接受门诊护理，看心理学家、社会工作者、精神科医生，但没有像其他医学领域那样同等水平的后续跟进。所以这是我们正在努力改进的地方。让我们谈谈技术。我知道你的很多工作都集中在技术在自杀预测和预防中的应用。告诉我们更多关于那项工作的信息。我的意思是，你做了什么样的研究，你发现了什么？在这里，我也感到非常兴奋。我认为由于正在开发的技术种类，这是我们理解、预测和预防自杀能力方面一个非常令人兴奋的时刻。所以我在这个领域工作了 25、30 年。在所有这些时间里，在很多年前，我知道之前，我们一直在某种程度上束手无策。抱歉，这是对你简短问题的迂回回答。束手无策，因为自杀有一些具有挑战性的方面。这是一种低基准率的行为。所以虽然它是主要死因，

46
